{
  "image_name": "2a8b7be70a8e35ae15821bb8c3bf53cf.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several major ways cancer cells increase MYC activity. Overall, it shows that MYC can be upregulated at the DNA, transcriptional, and protein levels, all converging on more MYC output.\n\nOn the left, genetic changes raise MYC expression directly. A chromosomal translocation places MYC next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain region, and gene amplification creates multiple MYC copies. Both routes lead to increased MYC mRNA and protein.\n\nIn the center, MYC is shown as a target of upstream signalling pathways. Membrane receptors and pathways including WNT, RTK, Notch, and TGFβ feed into the nucleus to control MYC transcription. Most arrows indicate activation of MYC expression, while TGFβ appears to act as an inhibitory input. The result is again elevated MYC mRNA and protein.\n\nOn the right, the figure shows post-translational control of MYC stability. Different phosphorylation states are associated with either instability or stability: phosphorylation around T58 is linked to degradation, whereas S62 is linked to a more stable MYC protein. Notes beside this panel suggest that increased ERK signaling and reduced PP2A, PIN1, or FBW7 favor stabilization. Stable MYC can partner with MAX on DNA, producing increased MYC protein without necessarily changing mRNA.",
